  OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM results WHERE id = 139 AND revision = 1
    AND statement_tex IS 'This picture shows an illustrative configuration for case (a) in the proof of the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound for the Taylor coefficients}. The solid line is $\Re s = \sigma$, the dashed line is $\Re s = \theta$, the strip between them is shaded, and $D_* = \sigma - \theta$. Here the poles nearest to $\sigma$ are $\rho_1$ and $\bar\rho_1$ (red), at the same distance $D$ (dashed circle), so their contributions to $A_k(\sigma)$ may cancel. The point $z = \sigma + i\tau$ is closer to $\rho_1$ (blue circle, radius $d$) than $\sigma$ is to any pole, and $\rho_1$ is the only pole at distance $d$ from $z$. The picture is not to scale: in the application to the \hyperref[8JP5]{interval criterion}, $\sigma \ge 200$, and all poles lie in $0 < \Re s < 1$.
\includegraphics[width=0.7, alt=Case a: the line Re s = sigma, the strip Re s greater than theta shaded, a blue circle around z touching the pole rho_1, and a dashed circle around sigma through rho_1 and its conjugate]{/static/figures/interval-criterion-a.svg}')
  OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM results WHERE id = 140 AND revision = 1
    AND statement_tex IS 'This picture shows an illustrative configuration for case (b) in the proof of the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound for the Taylor coefficients}. Here $D$ is the distance from $\sigma$ to the nearest pole (dashed circle), and every pole lies in $\Re s \le \sigma - D$, left of the dotted line. The nearest pole is then the real pole $\beta = \sigma - D$ (red), whose residue is positive. The picture is not to scale: in the application to the \hyperref[8JP5]{interval criterion}, $\sigma \ge 200$, and all poles lie in $0 < \Re s < 1$.
\includegraphics[width=0.7, alt=Case b: the dashed circle around sigma touches the real pole beta, and all poles lie left of the dotted line Re s = sigma minus D]{/static/figures/interval-criterion-b.svg}')
  OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM proofs WHERE id = 102 AND revision = 10
    AND body_tex IS '\emph{Idea.} The numbers $A_k(z) = \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}\mathcal H_\psi^{(k)}(z)$ are the Taylor coefficients of $w \mapsto \mathcal H_\psi(z - w)$. If $z$ has a unique nearest pole, at distance $d$, they grow like $d^{-k}$. Two parallel lemmas compare this growth on the line $\Re z = \sigma$ with $(\sigma - \theta)^{-k}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item the hypothesis caps it, by the \hyperref[8JT2]{one-sided bound for the Taylor coefficients};
\item a pole with $\Re\rho > \theta$ would exceed the cap, by the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound for the Taylor coefficients}.
\end{itemize}
Both lemmas bound the same quantity $|A_k(\sigma + i\tau)| + \Re A_k(\sigma)$, one from above and one from below. The upper bound works for this combination because it is at most $2\int_0^\infty g_+ K_k$, with $g(u) = \mathcal G_\psi(e^u)$, which involves only the positive part of $\mathcal G_\psi$: the hypothesis bounds $\mathcal G_\psi$ only from above.

\emph{The contradiction.} Suppose that some $\rho \in Z$ has $\Re\rho > \theta$. The hypothesis of the proposition is that of \ref{8JT2}; let $\sigma_0$ be as there, fix a real $\sigma \ge \sigma_0$, and let $C$ and $K$ be as there. Since $\sigma > 1$, \ref{8JT5} gives $\tau$, $c$, $d < \sigma - \theta$ and $k_0$. For every $k \in K$ with $k \ge k_0$,
\[ c\, d^{-k} \le |A_k(\sigma + i\tau)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \le C\, (\sigma - \theta)^{-k}, \]
so $\bigl((\sigma - \theta)/d\bigr)^{k} \le C/c$. Since $K$ is infinite and $(\sigma - \theta)/d > 1$, this fails for large $k \in K$. Hence $\Re\rho \le \theta$ for every $\rho \in Z$.')
  OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM results WHERE id = 141 AND revision = 8
    AND statement_tex IS 'Take $\psi = \mathbf 1$, the \hyperref[8JH4]{trivial character}, so that $L(s, \mathbf 1) = \zeta(s)$ and $o = 1$. The \hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} is $\mathcal C_{\mathbf 1} = \{\mathbf 1\}$ with weight $a_{\mathbf 1} = 1$, so the weighted sum is the \hyperref[8JF7]{smoothed prime sum} of the \hyperref[8JS9]{trivial character}:
\[ \mathcal G_{\mathbf 1}(x) = G_{\mathbf 1}(x) = \sum_{n \le x} \Lambda(n)\Bigl(\frac xn - 1\Bigr) - x\ln x + (1+\gamma)x. \]

\emph{Poles.} By \ref{8JS6} and \ref{8JN4}, the Mellin transform
\[ H_{\mathbf 1}(s) = -\frac{\zeta''(s)}{\zeta(s)}\,\frac{1}{s(s-1)} - \frac{1}{(s-1)^2} + \frac{1+\gamma}{s-1} \]
has a simple pole at each nontrivial zero $\rho$ of $\zeta$, with residue $-m(\rho)/(\rho(\rho-1))$, where $m(\rho)$ is the multiplicity. Moreover, \hyperref[8JUH]{none of these poles is real}. So for $\zeta$ only case (a) in the proof of the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound} can occur.

\emph{Explicit formula.} The \hyperref[8JUK]{residue theorem} gives, for $x \ge 1$,
\[ G_{\mathbf 1}(x) = \ln 2\pi - \sum_\rho \frac{x^\rho}{\rho(\rho - 1)} - \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{x^{-2n}}{2n(2n+1)}, \]
where $\rho$ runs over the nontrivial zeros of $\zeta$, counted with multiplicity. Each zero $\rho = \beta + i\gamma$ contributes an oscillation $x^\rho = x^\beta e^{i\gamma\ln x}$ of size $x^\beta$.

\emph{Taylor coefficients.} Since $\int_1^\infty x^\rho\, x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac{1}{s - \rho}$ for $\Re s > \Re\rho$, the \hyperref[8JS2]{Mellin transform} of the explicit formula is
\begin{align*}
H_{\mathbf 1}(s) = \frac{\ln 2\pi}{s} &- \sum_\rho \frac{1}{\rho(\rho-1)(s - \rho)} \\
&- \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{1}{2n(2n+1)(s + 2n)}.
\end{align*}
Hence, for $\Re z > 1$ and $k \ge 0$, the coefficients $A_k(z) = \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}H_{\mathbf 1}^{(k)}(z)$ are
\begin{align*}
A_k(z) = \frac{\ln 2\pi}{z^{k+1}} &- \sum_\rho \frac{1}{\rho(\rho-1)(z - \rho)^{k+1}} \\
&- \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{1}{2n(2n+1)(z + 2n)^{k+1}}.
\end{align*}
Each pole $p$ of $H_{\mathbf 1}$, with residue $c$, contributes $c/(z - p)^{k+1}$, so for large $k$ the poles nearest to $z$ dominate. The \hyperref[8JUP]{one-sided bound} uses the integral for $A_k$, and the \hyperref[8JUM]{lower bound} uses this series directly.

\emph{What the theorem says here.} For $\psi = \mathbf 1$ the \hyperref[8JK2]{interval estimate} only asks that $G_{\mathbf 1}(x) \le x^{1/2 + \varepsilon}$ on sparse intervals $[Y, Y^{3/2}]$. The \hyperref[8JUQ]{zeta interval criterion}, applied with $\theta=1/2+\varepsilon$ for every $0<\varepsilon<\frac12$, rules out nontrivial zeros to the right of the critical line. The functional equation then gives the Riemann hypothesis. Conversely, if the Riemann hypothesis holds, then $|x^\rho| = \sqrt x$ and $\rho(1 - \rho) = |\rho|^2$ for every zero, and $\sum_\rho \frac{1}{\rho(1-\rho)} = 2 + \gamma - \ln 4\pi \approx 0.0462$ \cite{Davenport}. With $\sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{1}{2n(2n+1)} = 1 - \ln 2 < 0.307$, the explicit formula then gives the much stronger two-sided bound
\[ |G_{\mathbf 1}(x) - \ln 2\pi| \le 0.0462\sqrt x + 0.307 \qquad (x \ge 1). \]

\emph{Graphs.} Panel (a) shows $G_{\mathbf 1}(x)$ for $1 \le x \le 10^7$, on a logarithmic $x$-axis, with this bound dashed.
\begin{center}
\textbf{(a)} $G_{\mathbf 1}(x)$ and the bound $\ln 2\pi \pm (0.0462\sqrt x + 0.307)$, for $1 \le x \le 10^7$
\end{center}
\includegraphics[width=1, alt=G_1(x) for x from 1 to 10 million on a logarithmic axis, oscillating with amplitude up to about 45, well inside the dashed curves ln 2 pi plus or minus (0.0462 sqrt x + 0.307)]{/static/figures/zeta-smoothed-sum.svg}
Panel (b) shows $(G_{\mathbf 1}(x) - \ln 2\pi)/\sqrt x$ for $10 \le x \le 10^7$, together with the explicit formula cut off after the first zero pair $\frac12 \pm 14.1347\,i$, which is a pure wave in $\ln x$, and after the first ten zero pairs, which already follows the data closely.
\begin{center}
\textbf{(b)} $\bigl(G_{\mathbf 1}(x) - \ln 2\pi\bigr)/\sqrt x$ and the explicit formula with the first one and ten zero pairs, for $10 \le x \le 10^7$
\end{center}
\includegraphics[width=1, alt=The normalized smoothed sum oscillating with size about 0.01 to 0.02, the first zero pair giving a pure wave and the first ten zero pairs following the data closely]{/static/figures/zeta-oscillation.svg}
Panel (c) shows $|A_k(\frac32 + i\tau)|$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$ and $0 \le \tau \le 50$, on a logarithmic scale; the dotted lines mark the heights $\gamma$ of the zeros $\frac12 + i\gamma$. Here $\sigma - \frac12 = 1$, so at $\tau = \gamma$ the zero $\rho = \frac12 + i\gamma$ contributes a term of size $1/|\rho|^2$ for every $k$, while the terms of the farther poles shrink as $k$ grows. For $k = 0$ and $1$ the pole at $s = 0$, with its comparatively large residue $\ln 2\pi \approx 1.84$, still dominates, and the zeros only make small dents. From $k = 2$ on, peaks of height about $1/|\rho|^2$ appear at the zeros. A zero $\beta + i\gamma$ with $\beta > \frac12$ would instead give peaks growing like $(\frac32 - \beta)^{-k}$, which is what the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound} detects.
\begin{center}
\textbf{(c)} $|A_k(\frac32 + i\tau)|$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$ and $0 \le \tau \le 50$; dotted lines at the zero heights $\gamma$
\end{center}
\includegraphics[width=1, alt=The absolute values of A_0 to A_3 on the line Re s = 3/2 for tau from 0 to 50 on a logarithmic scale, with peaks at the heights of the zeros of zeta appearing from k = 2 on]{/static/figures/zeta-taylor-coefficients.svg}')
